 The engine (`engine95.asm`) is one assembly whose PC-relative branches cross files; data inserted inside it can push a `bra.w`/`bsr.w` out of range. Put new data after the 2 MB fill.
 
+### PC-relative branches (branch out of range)
+
+SNASM’s **Branch (n bytes) is out of range** is the same ~±32 KB limit on **PC-relative** branches: **`bsr.w`**, **`bra.w`**, and **`bxx.w`**, not only `bra.w Begin` above.
+
+Two patterns that break mod builds:
+
+1. **New ROM inserted between existing modules** — Later includes move forward. The engine and other code may already use `.w` branches across modules with little margin; a small insert can push them past the limit.
+2. **Hooks into post-fill code** — Includes after the 2 MB `$FF` fill in `src/nhl95.asm` start at `$200000`. Calls from retail code into that region need **`jsr (label).l`** / **`jmp (label).l`**. Code in the new region should not **`bsr.w`** distant helpers; use **`.l`** or a **local `rts`**.
+
+When the build prints branch out of range, fix placement and call mode using this section before retargeting branches one at a time.
+
 ## Past 2 MB: backup RAM and bank switching
 
 The retail image is already 2 MB, so any growth means a 4 MB image whose second half overlaps the backup RAM window ($200001-$20FFFF). With ROM there, the console reads ROM instead of the save chip. A cartridge (or emulator) with the Sega mapper exposes the chip only while bit 0 of $A130F1 is set, so every save access in the save routines needs `move.b #1,($A130F1).l` before it and `clr.b ($A130F1).l` after it. That is a mod-branch change. Past 4 MB you need SSF2-style bank switching ($A130F3-$A130FF). `tools/fixrom.js` warns past 2 MB and refuses images over 4 MB.
diff --git a/tools/assembleHints.js b/tools/assembleHints.js
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..546b35e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/assembleHints.js
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+'use strict';
+
+// Helpers for SNASM68k assemble failures. Used by tools/build.js.
+
+const DEFAULT_DOC = 'docs/EXPANDING_THE_ROM.md';
+
+function filterAssemblerErrors(lines) {
+    return lines.filter((l) => /\.(asm|inc|mac)\(\d+|error|fatal|not recognised|undefined|could not/i.test(l) && !/error\(s\) from/.test(l)).slice(0, 40);
+}
+
+function hasBranchOutOfRange(lines) {
+    const text = (Array.isArray(lines) ? lines : [lines]).join('\n');
+    return /branch\s*\([^)]*\)\s*is out of range/i.test(text)
+        || (/out of range/i.test(text) && /branch/i.test(text));
+}
+
+function expandingRomBranchHint(options = {}) {
+    const doc = options.doc || DEFAULT_DOC;
+    const fill = options.fillHint || 'the stock ROM fill in the top-level .asm include list';
+    return [
+        '',
+        'Branch out of range (68000 PC-relative limit ~±32 KB):',
+        `  • Do not insert new ROM between existing modules if later code is branched to with .w — put new data after ${fill}.`,
+        '  • From retail code into post-fill code, use jsr (label).l / jmp (label).l.',
+        '  • In new post-fill code, use .l calls or a local rts instead of bsr.w to distant labels.',
+        `  • Read ${doc} (Short jumps — PC-relative branches).`,
+    ].join('\n');
+}
+
+function formatAssemblerFailureDetail(lines, options = {}) {
+    let detail = filterAssemblerErrors(lines).join('\n');
+    if (hasBranchOutOfRange(lines)) detail += expandingRomBranchHint(options);
+    return detail;
+}
+
+module.exports = {
+    DEFAULT_DOC,
+    filterAssemblerErrors,
+    hasBranchOutOfRange,
+    expandingRomBranchHint,
+    formatAssemblerFailureDetail,
+};
